                               OVERVIEW

shutdown-trigger is a bash script which will gently shutdown your
server connected to an Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS) when there
is a power failure.

It will be useful in the following scenario.

You have one or more servers powered by the UPS (typically at home).

The UPS has no support for direct communication with a connected
computer and when there is a power failure it only emits an acoustic
alarm.

This will be fine if you are nearby; but if the power failure happens
when you are not at home (or you are deeply sleeping) and lasts for a
long enough time, chances are that the UPS will exhaust its batteries
and your servers will be brutally killed; which, of course, is
something which we don't want to happen.

This script will detect a power failure condition and immediately (and
gently) shutdown your server(s) while they are still powered by the
batteries of the UPS.

Note: there are many UPS's which are able to communicate with the
devices attached to them, thus making a script like this useless. But,
in my experience, the costs of such UPS's are an order of magnitude
higher than those lacking any possibility to communicate with the
connected device(s). shutdown-trigger is handy when using these
latter, much cheaper UPS's.

How can shutdown-trigger detect a power failure condition? You need an
additional device (call it "trigger device") which:
1. is powered by the main line (that is: it is NOT connected to the
   UPS)
2. is connected to the server (typically at a USB port) and its
   connection can be polled by the server

So, the layout of the involved hardware is:


    +--------------------------------------------------+
    |              MAIN POWER LINE                     |
    +-------+-----------------------------+------------+
            |                             |
            |                             |
            |                        +----+-----+
            |                        |   UPS    |
            |                        +----+-----+
            |                             |              
            |                             |              
            |                             |              
   +--------+---------+    USB       +----+---------------+
   |  trigger device  +--------------+      server        |
   +------------------+              | (shutdown-trigger) |
                                     +--------------------+

This script runs as a daemon and periodically checks (typically with a
period of tens of seconds) for the connection of the trigger
device. If there is a power failure, the trigger device will die and
the check for its connection will fail: this is how shutdown-trigger
knows that it's time to shutdown the server.

The trigger device can be anything which can be brutally and safely
switched off by unplugging its power cable. I use a StarTech docking
station (https://www.startech.com/en-us/hdd/satdocku3s), but any other
(cheap) device satisfying requirements 1. and 2. above will do.

shutdown-trigger has support for shutting down not only the server
which it is running on, but also any other computer reachable from the
server on the same network. This is useful when you have multiple
servers powered by the UPS.

                        DEPENDENCIES

shutdown-trigger is a bash script, so you will need bash to run it
(https://gnu.org/s/bash/). It should run on any POSIX compliant
shell, but I did not check other shells, since I run bash on all my
machines.

The script uses standard unix commands like sed, grep etc: so you will
need those commands available (they are found on any unix system).


shutdown-trigger can be run as a daemon or as a client (of a
running shutdown-trigger daemon). The communication between the
daemon and the client is set up with the ncat command of the nmap
package (https://nmap.org/), which is thus a mandatory dependency.

It also uses the lsof command (https://github.com/lsof-org/lsof),
which is not always installed: you need that too.

                             INSTALLATION

Manual installation
  It is very easy to install shutdown-trigger by hand.

  - unpack the tarball
  - *edit* shutdown-trigger.conf.EDIT_ME, in particular the trigger_cmd
    and remote_host definitions (the latter if you need to shutdown more
    than one machine)
  - become superuser
  - copy shutdown-trigger.conf.EDIT_ME to /etc/shutdown-trigger.conf
  - copy the shutdown-trigger executable to a suitable system directory,
    eg /bin or /sbin
  - copy the man pages to their corresponding directories:
        shutdown-trigger.1 ->      .../man/man1
        shutdown-trigger.conf.5 -> .../man/man5

Using the makefile
  - unpack the tarball
  - *edit* shutdown-trigger.conf.EDIT_ME, in particular the trigger_cmd
    and remote_host definitions (the latter if you need to shutdown more
    than one machine)
  - browse (and possibly edit) the definitions of BINDIR MAN1 MAN5 and
    ETC in Makefile
  - become superuser
  - in the top directory of the unpacked tarball issue:
       make install
